  "question": {
    "text": "$\\text { The function } f:(-\\infty, \\infty) \\rightarrow(-\\infty, 1) \\text {, defined by } f(x)=\\frac{2^x-2^{-x}}{2^x+2^{-x}} \\text { is : }$",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "H7rED",
      "text": "$\\text { Neither one-one nor onto }$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "fDp6F",
      "text": "$\\text { Onto but not one-one }$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "3zzyN",
      "text": "$\\text { Both one-one and onto }$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "ugsuX",
      "text": "$\\text { One-one but not onto }$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\frac{2^{2 \\mathrm{x}}-1}{2^{2 \\mathrm{x}}+1} \\\\\n& =1-\\frac{2}{2^{2 \\mathrm{x}}+1} \\\\\n& \\mathrm{f}^{\\prime}(\\mathrm{x})=\\frac{2}{\\left(2^{2 \\mathrm{x}}+1\\right)^2} \\cdot 2.2^{2 \\mathrm{x}} \\cdot \\ln 2 \\text { i.e always }+\\mathrm{ve}\n\\end{aligned}$\nso $f(x)$ is $\\uparrow$ function\n$\\begin{aligned}\n& \\therefore \\mathrm{f}(-\\infty)=-1 \\\\\n& \\mathrm{f}(\\infty)=1 \\\\\n& \\therefore \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x}) \\in(-1,1) \\neq \\text { co-domain }\n\\end{aligned}$\nso function is one-one but not onto",
    "image": null
  },
